# Checkout line endings are part of what CI executes, so they are pinned here
# rather than left to each machine's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Three failures in this repository came from this: a Windows checkout that
# converts a shell script to CRLF cannot run it (`bash` rejects the trailing
# `\r` on the first real line), a workflow file read as text no longer matches
# the step markers the release tests look for, and the dashboard bundle rebuilt
# in place stopped matching the CRLF checkout it replaced. The first two were
# worked around in the reader; this file removes the cause of all three.
#
# PowerShell is deliberately not listed: Windows PowerShell and PowerShell 7
# accept either ending, and a CRLF checkout is what a Windows author expects.
# Binary assets are not listed either, so nothing is renormalised by accident.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.nsi  text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.go   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
# `vite build` writes the embedded dashboard into the version-controlled
# internal/gateway/assets, replacing files that are committed as build output. A
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true would materialise the bundle with CRLF and the
# rebuild would write LF back: git compares the same normalised bytes, but the
# on-disk size differs, so `git status --porcelain` reports a modification and
# `radanor-version -release` refuses the exact `vX.Y.Z` tag it was asked to
# build. Pinning the bundle to LF makes the rebuild byte-identical everywhere.
*.js   text eol=lf
